  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nVery comfortable and pleasant conditions return today into\ntomorrow as high pressure builds over the Northeast. Then, a\nBermuda high becomes anchored off the East Coast for the end of\nthe week. Persistent southwesterly flow around its periphery \nwill advect a warm and very humid air mass from the Gulf and \nwestern Atlantic into the Northeast with PWATs reaching 2 ST DEV\nabove normal per the NAEFS and ranking above the 90th percentile\nof the CFSR climatology for early August. A weak shortwave from\nthe Southeast U.S becomes sheared out as it tracks within the \nsouthwest flow aloft into the Northeast with broad troughing \nbecoming established over the Canadian Prairie and the Great \nLakes. Southwest winds aloft strengthen as the Northeast is\ncaught between the upstream troughing and Bermuda high off \nshore supporting weak deep layer shear. The moist air mass in\nthe presence of at least some sunshine will easily contribute to\nincreased instability. Mid and upper level forcing is weak but\nthere are signs of a sfc pressure trough tracking eastward off \nLake Ontario which would provide a focus for sfc-based \nconvection on Thursday. Therefore, expecting isolated to \nscattered showers and thunderstorms to develop during the \ntraditional peak heating hours capable of producing heavy \ndownpours. Shower/storm coverage will increase should the sfc \ntrough push eastward. Favorable instability and sufficient deep \nlayer shear gives us a low chance for strong/severe storms but \nwarm mid-levels/weak lapse rates may hinder updraft strengthen.\nSimilar set-up for Friday but shower/storm coverage looks to \nincrease as the upstream troughing and sfc boundary pushes \neastward. These factors can also support a greater risk for \nsevere weather. Otherwise, WPC maintains a widespread marginal \nrisk for excessive rainfall both days given potential for heavy\ndownpours where soils are saturated from recent heavy rain.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nThe combination of uncomfortable humidity levels and temperatures\nwarming slightly above normal Thursday and Friday increases the\nrisk for heat-related illness, especially in valley areas. \nLatest HeatRisk graphics show much of eastern NY and western \nNew England reaching the minor category on Thursday with minor\nto moderate on Friday. Latest NBM probabilistic guidance shows a\n40 to 60% chance for temperatures exceeding 85 degrees both days\nin valley areas with a 60 to 90% chance in the mid-Hudson\nValley. There is even a 15 to 30% chance that the mid-Hudson \nValley reaches or exceeds 90 degrees. There is a low to medium \nchance that these conditions will necessitate heat advisories \nwith the highest chance in the mid-Hudson Valley. Plan to avoid \nstrenuous outdoor activities both days, have a way to stay cool \nor access air conditioning and drink plenty of water, even if \nyou are not thirsty.",
